  "final_text": "Both objects exist and the rule is enabled.\n\n- **Dashboard:** \"EFF-986ef3f8 Web logs overview\" (id `677b79c3-1ec7-4882-b936-20da43af948d`) has 6 panels: total requests, average response bytes, unique client IPs, 5xx responses, requests over time, and requests by response code. I read it back and the panel count and titles match.\n- **Rule:** \"EFF-986ef3f8 5xx spike\" (id `20ef4b35-ba14-445c-8187-485d7b7a666a`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every 1m, counts documents where `response.keyword: 5*` over the last 5m, and fires when the count is greater than 10. After one minute its status was `ok` with no error.\n\nChoices I made:\n- The 5xx filter is a KQL wildcard on `response.keyword`. The 5xx panel uses an ES|QL `TO_INTEGER(response)` range instead. Both should match the same documents, but I did not compare their counts.\n- The rule uses the `stackAlerts` consumer and has no connector or action attached, so it will evaluate but notify nobody. Say if you want an action added.\n\nLimitations:\n- Elasticsearch isn't reachable directly, and Kibana's search proxy endpoints are disabled here. So I explored the fields from the data view metadata only, without looking at sample values or running ad hoc queries.\n- I have not confirmed that any of the panels render data or that the rule has seen any 5xx traffic. The sample data is static, so the rule may not fire unless the data falls inside its 5-minute window.",
